WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:06.222
Meet SlimFaas! This CNCF Sandbox project
lets idle agent tools scale to zero for

00:00:06.222 --> 00:00:11.299
greener Kubernetes workloads. Already have
an HTTP app on Kubernetes? You can

00:00:11.299 --> 00:00:15.952
integrate it in minutes. Route calls
through SlimFaas. Your application code

00:00:15.952 --> 00:00:20.183
stays independent, in any language, with
no driver to install.

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:25.153
First up: synchronous functions over HTTP.
Let's wake one up!

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:32.043
Find Fibonacci on the right. Zero ready
replicas. Its HTTP address is already

00:00:32.043 --> 00:00:32.500
available.

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:38.733
The HTTP proxy receives the call and wakes
the deployment from zero to its configured

00:00:38.733 --> 00:00:44.067
starting count. Here, that's one pod. As
soon as it's ready, the proxy forwards the

00:00:44.067 --> 00:00:46.200
call and the caller gets fifty-five.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:51.772
Now watch the idle timer. The last pod
goes to sleep, ready for the next call.

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:56.796
Next up: asynchronous queues over HTTP.
Here comes the burst!

00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:02.626
Thirty HTTP requests ask for Fibonacci of
forty-eight. All thirty get an accepted

00:01:02.626 --> 00:01:05.657
response while the work waits in the
queue.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:10.938
Once a pod is running, SlimFaas scrapes
Prometheus-format metrics itself and

00:01:10.938 --> 00:01:16.458
evaluates the scaling rule locally. Here,
queue depth takes us from one worker to

00:01:16.458 --> 00:01:18.824
four, without a separate Prometheus
server.

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:24.895
The queue gives Kubernetes time to scale.
All thirty calculations finish. With the

00:01:24.895 --> 00:01:27.665
backlog empty, the workers return to zero.

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:32.909
Publish and subscribe over HTTP: one event
for every replica!

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:38.985
Our agent system has two evaluators and
two audit pods. Each keeps its own tool

00:01:38.985 --> 00:01:41.039
catalog. A tool has just changed.

00:01:41.950 --> 00:01:47.590
Publish once. Follow those paths! All four
pods receive the HTTP event and discard

00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:48.798
their stale copy.

00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:55.230
Now let's pass an agent result through a
durable Stream over HTTP.

00:01:56.150 --> 00:02:01.668
One HTTP append gives the evaluator and
the audit function their own copy to

00:02:01.668 --> 00:02:04.427
process. Each function tracks its own
progress.

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:09.608
One replica per function handles this
attempt. Both subscriptions acknowledge

00:02:09.608 --> 00:02:15.569
sequence one, with zero lag. Retries are
possible, so make handlers safe to repeat.

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:23.209
There's more! Submit a job over HTTP or by
cron. SlimFaas queues runs until a slot

00:02:23.209 --> 00:02:28.009
opens. You can also schedule a function to
wake before the morning rush.

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:34.111
Cache a tool result with Sets. Post a
value under a key over HTTP, set its

00:02:34.111 --> 00:02:37.014
expiry, then read it back on the next
call.

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:44.221
With Files, stream bytes in over HTTP and
pass the key to the next agent. A few

00:02:44.221 --> 00:02:49.016
kilobytes or hundreds of megabytes? Same
route! Stream the artifact back before it

00:02:49.016 --> 00:02:49.385
expires.

00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:55.519
Watch the queue drain as the workers come
online. That's what the Live Stream

00:02:55.519 --> 00:03:00.366
dashboard lets you see at a glance!
SlimFaas also has OpenTelemetry built in.

00:03:00.366 --> 00:03:05.212
Enable OTLP export to send traces,
metrics, and logs to your own collector.

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:10.374
Give the local demo a spin and watch your
own HTTP tool wake up!
